<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Page
    x:Class="WinZ3805A.Views.SettingsPage"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
    xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
    xmlns:tk="using:CommunityToolkit.WinUI.Controls"
    xmlns:controls="using:WinZ3805A.Controls"
    mc:Ignorable="d">

    <!--
        Settings, per §10.13.

        The section did not exist when this page was built: §9.7.5 gave it Ctrl+comma, §10.11 said
        the console is "enabled in Settings - Advanced", and DetailsDestinations cited a "§10.13"
        that had never been written. It was filed rather than invented, and §10.13 was written the
        same day (#146).

        §10.13.1 is why this page carries one section. Poll cadences are deliberately not offered -
        §7.3 fixes them and §12 gives the poller sole ownership, so making them user-visible is an
        amendment to two sections rather than a feature. The display time zone lives on the main
        window and §10.14; units are unspecified. The page says so on screen rather than showing
        controls that do nothing.

        REWRITTEN 30 Aug 2026 (#320) onto SettingsCard and SettingsExpander. §9.10.1 has always
        named them and the package had been referenced and unused. The shape they replace was a
        card per GROUP holding a stack of ToggleSwitch-then-caption pairs, in which a switch and the
        paragraph explaining it were siblings rather than parent and child: nothing in the markup
        said which caption belonged to which control, and the answer was proximity. A SettingsCard
        makes that structural - Header, Description and the control are one row - and brings the
        keyboard behaviour and the narrow-pane wrap that this page would otherwise hand-roll.

        The GROUP is now a heading with rows under it rather than a bordered box, which is the shape
        the control is built for and the one Windows Settings uses. The chrome is still §9's: see
        WzSettingsCardStyle, which paints these the same surface as every other card here.
    -->
    <ScrollViewer HorizontalScrollMode="Disabled" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
        <StackPanel controls:CardEntrance.IsEnabled="True"
                    Padding="{ThemeResource WzPageMarginThickness}"
                    MaxWidth="{ThemeResource WzContentMaxWidth}"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                    Spacing="{ThemeResource WzSpaceXl}">

            <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource WzTitleTextStyle}" Text="Settings" />

            <!-- ================================ Advanced =============================== -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="{ThemeResource WzSpaceXs}">
                <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource WzBodyStrongTextStyle}" Text="Advanced" />

                <!--
                    HorizontalAlignment IS load-bearing here and on the two paragraphs below. A
                    MaxWidth-constrained element in a Stretch slot centres itself in what is left
                    over, so this line and the description under "Undocumented read-only queries"
                    both sat indented into the middle of the page while every other row started at
                    the card's left edge — measured on screen, not predicted.
                -->
                <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource WzBodyTextStyle}"
                           Foreground="{ThemeResource WzTextSecondaryBrush}"
                           MaxWidth="{ThemeResource WzProseMaxWidth}"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           Margin="{ThemeResource WzSpaceXxsThickness}"
                           Text="For working out what the receiver is doing, rather than for using it." />

                <tk:SettingsCard Style="{ThemeResource WzSettingsCardStyle}"
                                 Header="Advanced Console"
                                 Description="Adds a page below Settings offering every command in the catalog as a picker, with a transcript of everything sent and received. It changes what is reachable, never what is permitted: the commands this application will not send are absent from the catalog, so they are absent from the console.">
                    <ToggleSwitch x:Name="ConsoleSwitch"
                                  OnContent="Shown"
                                  OffContent="Hidden"
                                  AutomationProperties.Name="Show the Advanced Console"
                                  Toggled="OnConsoleToggled" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <!--
                    §8.5's own sentence, verbatim, which #56 requires the toggle to carry. It is
                    quoted rather than paraphrased because every clause of it is a warning: the
                    queries are undocumented, they may answer with nonsense, and - the part a user
                    most needs - nothing is being set.

                    Two paragraphs rather than one Description string, so the verbatim sentence stays
                    identifiable as a quotation and the second note stays a separate statement about
                    where the queries appear. Description takes an object, so this is what it is for.
                -->
                <tk:SettingsCard Style="{ThemeResource WzSettingsCardStyle}"
                                 Header="Undocumented read-only queries">
                    <tk:SettingsCard.Description>
                        <StackPanel Spacing="{ThemeResource WzSpaceXxs}">
                            <TextBlock MaxWidth="{ThemeResource WzProseMaxWidth}"
                                       HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                       Text="Enable undocumented read-only queries. These are present in the receiver's command parser but absent from the published manual. They may return errors or nonsense. No setting is changed." />
                            <TextBlock MaxWidth="{ThemeResource WzProseMaxWidth}"
                                       HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                       Text="Six queries appear on the Diagnostics page, each run only when you click it. There is no way to type one, and the set forms of these nodes are excluded permanently and cannot be enabled here or anywhere else." />
                        </StackPanel>
                    </tk:SettingsCard.Description>
                    <ToggleSwitch x:Name="ExperimentalSwitch"
                                  OnContent="Shown"
                                  OffContent="Hidden"
                                  AutomationProperties.Name="Enable undocumented read-only queries"
                                  Toggled="OnExperimentalToggled" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>
            </StackPanel>

            <!-- ============================== Appearance =============================== -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="{ThemeResource WzSpaceXs}">
                <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource WzBodyStrongTextStyle}" Text="Appearance" />

                <!--
                    §9.4.2 in the user's terms. What the description does not say is as deliberate as
                    what it does: it never promises the app will look "correct" either way, because
                    both are correct. The reason to prefer the built-in accent is stated as a fact
                    about the severity colours rather than as a recommendation, so a user who wants
                    their desktop accent is not being told they are wrong.
                -->
                <tk:SettingsCard Style="{ThemeResource WzSettingsCardStyle}"
                                 Header="Use the Windows accent colour"
                                 Description="Selected items, buttons and links follow the accent you set in Windows. The colours that mean caution and critical never change - they are chosen to stay distinct from the built-in accent, so a Windows accent in the same family as one of them is harder to tell apart at a glance. You will be told if the one you have picked is close.">
                    <ToggleSwitch x:Name="SystemAccentSwitch"
                                  OnContent="Windows"
                                  OffContent="This app&apos;s own"
                                  AutomationProperties.Name="Use the Windows accent colour"
                                  Toggled="OnSystemAccentToggled" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>

                <!--
                    Anchored to the switch rather than shown as a dialog. The user has just made a
                    choice and is looking at the control they made it with; a modal would interrupt
                    to say something they can act on without leaving the page - and §9.4.2 makes this
                    advice, not a permission gate.
                -->
                <TeachingTip x:Name="CollisionTip"
                             Target="{x:Bind SystemAccentSwitch}"
                             Title="This accent is close to a warning colour"
                             PreferredPlacement="Bottom"
                             IsLightDismissEnabled="False"
                             ActionButtonContent="Use the app&apos;s accent"
                             ActionButtonClick="OnRevertAccent"
                             CloseButtonContent="Keep the Windows accent"
                             CloseButtonClick="OnKeepAccent" />
            </StackPanel>

            <!-- ================================ Alerts ================================= -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="{ThemeResource WzSpaceXs}">
                <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource WzBodyStrongTextStyle}" Text="Alerts" />

                <tk:SettingsCard Style="{ThemeResource WzSettingsCardStyle}"
                                 Header="Tell me when the receiver loses GPS lock"
                                 Description="A Windows notification when the receiver stops disciplining to GPS, and one when it starts again. Brief drop-outs are not reported: a loss has to last a minute before it is worth telling you about, and a receiver that recovers inside that minute is never mentioned at all.">
                    <ToggleSwitch x:Name="LockNotificationsSwitch"
                                  OnContent="On"
                                  OffContent="Off"
                                  AutomationProperties.Name="Notify when the receiver loses GPS lock"
                                  Toggled="OnLockNotificationsToggled" />
                </tk:SettingsCard>
            </StackPanel>

            <!--
                §10.3's close behaviour (#280). Its own group rather than a row under Alerts: this is
                about whether the application is running at all, which is a larger claim than whether
                it speaks up.

                A SettingsExpander and not three flat cards, because these three are not peers. Only
                the first is a decision on its own; starting hidden and quitting outright both mean
                nothing until the application keeps running, and the expander is the control that
                says so structurally rather than by the order they happen to be listed in.
            -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="{ThemeResource WzSpaceXs}">
                <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource WzBodyStrongTextStyle}" Text="Running in the background" />

                <tk:SettingsExpander Style="{ThemeResource WzSettingsExpanderStyle}"
                                     Header="Keep running when I close the window"
                                     Description="On, closing the window leaves the receiver being polled, so the trend keeps filling and the alerts above still reach you. Click the notification area icon to bring the window back, or right-click it and choose Exit. Off, closing the window exits and monitoring stops with it."
                                     IsExpanded="True">
                    <tk:SettingsExpander.Content>
                        <ToggleSwitch x:Name="KeepRunningSwitch"
                                      OnContent="On"
                                      OffContent="Off"
                                      AutomationProperties.Name="Keep running when the window is closed"
                                      Toggled="OnKeepRunningToggled" />
                    </tk:SettingsExpander.Content>

                    <tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
                        <tk:SettingsCard Header="Start in the notification area">
                            <tk:SettingsCard.Description>
                                <TextBlock x:Name="StartMinimisedNote"
                                           MaxWidth="{ThemeResource WzProseMaxWidth}"
                                           HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                           Text="Starts with no window, monitoring from the notification area." />
                            </tk:SettingsCard.Description>
                            <ToggleSwitch x:Name="StartMinimisedSwitch"
                                          OnContent="On"
                                          OffContent="Off"
                                          AutomationProperties.Name="Start in the notification area with no window"
                                          Toggled="OnStartMinimisedToggled" />
                        </tk:SettingsCard>

                        <!--
                            An exit that does not depend on the notification area (#280).

                            The tray menu is the documented way out once the close button stops
                            exiting - but Windows 11 does not promote a newly registered tray icon.
                            It goes into the hidden overflow, and a user who has not dragged it out
                            cannot right-click what they cannot see. Leaving the tray as the ONLY
                            exit would mean an application quittable in principle and, on a fresh
                            install, by Task Manager in practice.

                            So the application can always be quit from its own surface. §13's
                            acceptance asks for a discoverable, non-Task-Manager way out, and this is
                            the one that does not depend on shell behaviour nobody here controls.

                            Inside the expander with the toggle that creates the problem, which is
                            where it is looked for: a user reading "closing the window will not quit"
                            has the other way out on the next line.

                            The button's label is set in code from Package.Current.DisplayName
                            (§6.3): the product name is never hard-coded in XAML, because identity
                            and display name are decoupled deliberately and a rename must stay a
                            one-line change. The card's own header is set there for the same reason.
                        -->
                        <tk:SettingsCard x:Name="ExitCard"
                                         Description="Stops polling and closes the application, whether or not it is set to keep running.">
                            <Button x:Name="ExitButton"
                                    Click="OnExitClicked"
                                    ToolTipService.ToolTip="Stop monitoring and close the application" />
                        </tk:SettingsCard>
                    </tk:SettingsExpander.Items>
                </tk:SettingsExpander>
            </StackPanel>

            <!-- ============================== Not built yet ============================ -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="{ThemeResource WzSpaceXs}">
                <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource WzBodyStrongTextStyle}" Text="Elsewhere for now" />

                <Border Style="{ThemeResource WzCardStyle}">
                    <TextBlock Style="{ThemeResource WzBodyTextStyle}"
                               Foreground="{ThemeResource WzTextSecondaryBrush}"
                               MaxWidth="{ThemeResource WzProseMaxWidth}"
                               TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               Text="Poll cadences, the display time zone and units are not on this page yet. The time zone is chosen from the main window's clock; the poll cadences are fixed at the intervals the specification sets." />
                </Border>
            </StackPanel>
        </StackPanel>
    </ScrollViewer>
</Page>
